# Who must approve what in this repo.
#
# The default is a human on @thegoodparty/gp-contrib: `*` below owns every
# path, and the opt-out list after it is the entire set of paths that can
# merge on a bot approval. The ruleset on the default branch requires one
# approving review and code-owner review, and a path with no owner satisfies
# the code-owner half vacuously, so a pattern with no owners opts a path out.
#
# Deliberately opt-out rather than opt-in. An opt-in list has to be extended
# every time a tree is added, and forgetting is silent: a new Pulumi project
# or a new credentialled workflow lands with no human behind it. With `*`
# owned, adding a directory cannot leave it unowned. The cost moves to the
# other side and fails safe -- a new tree that should merge on a bot approval
# needs an opt-out line, and until it has one it needs a human.
#
# gp-contrib is used because it is the only team with write access to this
# repo; gp-secops has read only, and GitHub silently ignores code owners
# without write access.
#
# ## The test for an opt-out
#
# Ask what credential the path can reach, not whether it is application code.
# A path belongs on the opt-out list only if nothing in it -- and nothing that
# reads or executes it -- can change what a credential is allowed to do, or
# what code runs with one. When in doubt, leave it owned: the whole point of
# the default is that the safe answer is the one you get by not acting.
#
# ## The opt-outs
#
# `/docs/`, `/README.md` -- documentation.
#
# `/.gitignore` -- inert.
#
# `/CLAUDE.md` and `**/CLAUDE.md` -- removed from the review list by #173,
# preserved here. The pattern fired on routine documentation rather than on
# changes to the reviewer's judgement, and the reviewer is told to read
# CODEOWNERS as the authority rather than any summary. The root file is the
# one the default would otherwise re-own.
#
# Everything else is owned by the default. The trees that define the posture
# are the ones to keep in mind when reviewing an opt-out request:
#
# - `.github/` -- all of it. OIDC trust keys on the ref, not the filename, so
#   a workflow *added* on main can assume the deploy roles.
# - `delegate/` -- the framework, the lambda dispatcher, the worker entrypoint
#   and the subagent prompts all shape what the reviewer does, and every
#   delegate agent shares one worker runtime and one GitHub App installation
#   token with write access to the product repos. Letting the reviewer's
#   approval merge a change to the reviewer collapses the boundary between
#   coder and reviewer.
# - `deploy/`, `deploy-org/`, `deploy-workbench/`, `deploy-infrastructure/` --
#   every Pulumi project, not just the role and permission-set files. A new
#   component that mints an IAM role is one `index.ts` import away.
# - `utils/` -- `deploy/components/identity-center/policies.ts` imports
#   `bedrockInvokeResources` from `utils/bedrock-models.ts`, and the account
#   ids in `utils/accounts.ts` feed the same permission set's resource ARNs.
#   Those read like constants files and edit like IAM policy.
# - `scripts/`, `run-script.ts` -- `deploy-workbench.yml` triggers on
#   `scripts/enable-bedrock-models.ts` and runs it under a deploy role; that
#   script accepts model provider terms on GoodParty's behalf, and
#   `run-script.ts` dynamically imports anything in `scripts/`.
# - `package.json`, `package-lock.json`, `tsconfig.json`, `.dockerignore` --
#   one `package.json` builds every image, so a dependency added for any one
#   service also installs into the delegate worker that holds the GitHub App
#   token; `tsconfig.json` governs the same `tsc` that compiles `delegate/`;
#   `.dockerignore` is the build context for the worker image.
#
# ## Rules for editing this list
#
# Scope by directory. A file-scoped opt-out leaves the same hole one file
# over: opting out `deploy/components/evil.ts` says nothing about the next
# file added beside it. This repo has already been bitten by exactly that --
# see the `job_workflow_ref` comment in
# `deploy/components/ci-roles/policies.ts`. The file-scoped entries below are
# repository-root files, which have no directory to scope by.
#
# Before adding a path to the opt-out side, check what executes it. The
# ordering in `deploy.yml` -- install, test and build strictly before the
# credentials step -- is load-bearing for every opt-out path in this repo, and
# is the only reason an unowned test file is not an admin-credentialled shell.
#
# Widening this list is a security change. It is also, by construction, a
# change to a path the default owns, so it needs a human.

*                                @thegoodparty/gp-contrib

/docs/
/README.md
/.gitignore
/CLAUDE.md
**/CLAUDE.md
